WEBVTT - Most Fascinating Stories - Episode 56: The Great Chicago Fire and the Scapegoat of Mrs. O'Leary

1
00:00:03.040 --> 00:00:05.920
[Rebecca] Welcome back to Most Fascinating Stories.

2
00:00:05.920 --> 00:00:07.200
[Rebecca] I'm Rebecca.

3
00:00:07.200 --> 00:00:15.760
[Rebecca] On the night of October 8, 1871, a small blaze ignited in a wooden barn on Decoven Street on the west side of Chicago.

4
00:00:15.760 --> 00:00:26.080
[Rebecca] Fanned by Gale Force winds from the southwest and fueled by an entire city constructed of pine timber, the fire grew into a roaring apocalyptic firestorm.

5
00:00:26.080 --> 00:00:35.040
[Rebecca] Over 30 hours, it incinerated three and a half square miles, leveled 17,000 buildings, and left 100,000 people homeless.

6
00:00:35.040 --> 00:00:43.600
[Rebecca] But even as the ashes smoldered, journalists invented a sensational lie that blamed an impoverished Irish immigrant widow and her cow.

7
00:00:43.600 --> 00:00:50.640
[Rebecca] George brings us the true, tragic story of the Great Chicago Fire.

8
00:01:27.840 --> 00:01:33.440
[George] But beneath this veneer of bustling commerce lay an unimaginable structural death trap.

9
00:01:33.440 --> 00:01:37.680
[George] The entire metropolis was constructed almost entirely of wood.

10
00:01:37.680 --> 00:01:49.280
[George] 59,000 homes, churches, grain elevators, lumber yards, and factories built of cheap, highly resinous white pine harvested from northern Wisconsin and Michigan forests.

11
00:01:49.280 --> 00:01:52.160
[George] Even the municipal infrastructure was kindling.

12
00:01:52.160 --> 00:02:02.200
[George] Sixty miles of wooden pine block paved streets, five hundred miles of wooden boardwalk sidewalks, and dry pine shingles covering nearly every private roof.

13
00:02:02.200 --> 00:02:07.880
[George] To compound the danger, that summer and autumn had produced the worst drought in Midwestern history.

14
00:02:07.880 --> 00:02:16.040
[George] For fourteen consecutive weeks, blistering sun had baked the prairie, reducing pine boards and roof timbers to bone-dry tinder.

15
00:02:16.040 --> 00:02:28.600
[George] Minor fires had erupted daily across the city for a week, leaving Chicago exhausted, under-equipped fire departments suffering from burst hoses, damaged steam pumps, and bone weary horses.

16
00:02:28.600 --> 00:02:40.520
[George] Piles of pine sawdust and dry timber shavings littered the alleys between wooden cottages, while high winds off the prairie sucked all humidity from the air in a catastrophic meteorological setup.

17
00:02:40.520 --> 00:02:55.720
[George] City leaders had repeatedly ignored urgent warnings from municipal fire wardens, prioritizing rapid commercial expansion and cheap construction over building codes and public safety, creating an immense powder keg awaiting only a single spark.

18
00:02:55.720 --> 00:03:07.560
[George] The fire department possessed only 17 horse-drawn steam pumpers and less than 50 miles of usable hose for the entire city, an utterly inadequate defense against an impending catastrophe.

19
00:03:07.560 --> 00:03:20.680
[George] At approximately 9 o'clock on Sunday evening, October 8, flames were spotted erupting from the wooden barn behind the humble cottage of Patrick and Catherine O'Leary at 137 DeCoven Street.

20
00:03:20.680 --> 00:03:35.080
[George] Catherine O'Leary was an industrious, deeply religious Irish immigrant woman who supported her family by operating a small neighborhood dairy, keeping six cows in her barn and delivering fresh milk daily to local working class households.

21
00:03:35.080 --> 00:03:46.000
[George] Catherine and her husband had already gone to bed for the night after an exhausting day of farm chores when neighbor Dennis Sullivan spotted smoke from across the street and ran shouting into the yard.

22
00:03:46.000 --> 00:03:54.400
[George] Sullivan rushed heroically into the burning barn to rescue the livestock, suffering severe burns as flaming rafters fell from the roof.

23
00:03:54.400 --> 00:04:05.360
[George] While the exact cause of the initial spark remains an unsolved historical mystery, likely ignited by careless neighborhood men smoking in the alley, an overturned clay pipe.

24
00:04:05.360 --> 00:04:13.360
[George] Or spontaneous combustion of dry hay, the dry pine timber of the barn burst into a roaring column of flame within minutes.

25
00:04:13.360 --> 00:04:24.240
[George] Catherine O'Leary was a hardworking, devout woman who labored twelve hours a day tending her livestock, completely unaware that an alley prowler or stray match would ignite her barn.

26
00:04:24.240 --> 00:04:31.520
[George] In later years, journalists admitted that Catherine was fast asleep in bed and had nothing to do with starting the blaze.

27
00:04:31.520 --> 00:04:45.040
[George] The O'Leary cottage itself was miraculously spared by a shifting eddy in the wind, standing as an untouched wooden island in a landscape of total black and ruin, while the inferno raced unchecked toward the heart of the city.

28
00:04:45.040 --> 00:04:56.000
[George] The wooden fence surrounding the O'Leary Yard had caught flame within moments, throwing tongues of fire toward nearby lumber sheds as neighboring families rushed into the streets in nightclothes.

29
00:04:56.000 --> 00:05:05.360
[George] What might have remained an isolated neighborhood fire was transformed into a municipal catastrophe by a disastrous sequence of human and mechanical failures.

30
00:05:05.360 --> 00:05:13.840
[George] The first neighborhood alarm box failed to transmit the telegraph signal to the central courthouse bell tower due to a faulty electrical connection.

31
00:05:13.840 --> 00:05:20.000
[George] When watchman Matthew's Shaffer finally spotted the crimson glow from the courthouse cupola a mile away.

32
00:05:20.000 --> 00:05:29.920
[George] He misjudged the direction in the smoke-choked night and telegraphed the wrong box number to the engine companies, sending fire pumpers galloping two miles south in the wrong direction.

33
00:05:30.760 --> 00:05:42.600
[George] By the time Fire Marshal Robert Williams and his horse-drawn steam engines arrived on De Coven Street, the fire had already consumed half a dozen wooden cottages and jumped an eighty-foot street.

34
00:05:42.600 --> 00:05:52.440
[George] The southwest wind had increased to gale force, 30 to 40 miles an hour, driving a river of blowing embers directly toward the commercial heart of the city.

35
00:05:52.440 --> 00:06:03.880
[George] Firefighters who had spent 36 hours battling blazes the previous night dragged their heavy hose carts through the dusty streets, their faces blistered and eyes burning from sulphurous smoke.

36
00:06:03.880 --> 00:06:13.160
[George] When the pumpers connected to street hydrants, water pressure plummeted because the city pumping station was already overtaxed by prolonged drought conditions.

37
00:06:13.160 --> 00:06:27.160
[George] The clang of the giant courthouse bell rang across the river until the bell tower itself caught fire, signaling to terrified citizens that organized firefighting had broken down completely and that the entire city was in mortal peril.

38
00:06:27.160 --> 00:06:38.840
[George] Telegraph lines snapped under falling branches and scorching heat, plunging entire precincts into darkness and cutting off communication between the district marshals and the central alarm office.

39
00:06:38.840 --> 00:06:43.800
[George] At 11 o'clock, the disaster reached an unstoppable tipping point.

40
00:06:43.800 --> 00:06:57.000
[George] The intense thermal updraft formed a convective firestorm, sucking surrounding air inward with hurricane intensity and generating superheated fire whirlwinds that spun through city streets like flaming tornadoes.

41
00:06:57.000 --> 00:07:08.680
[George] Embers the size of cedar shingles were carried half a mile high over the south branch of the Chicago River, landing upon the wooden roofs of tar paper factories and coal yards on the opposite bank.

42
00:07:08.680 --> 00:07:16.320
[George] The river, thick with grease, oil slicks, and floating timber from lumber docks, failed completely as a firebreak.

43
00:07:14.760 --> 00:07:20.800
[George] The river itself appeared to catch fire as burning debris coated the surface.

44
00:07:21.040 --> 00:07:30.240
[George] Structures previously considered fireproof, grand limestone banks, marble hotels and brick warehouses exploded from radiant heat.

45
00:07:30.240 --> 00:07:39.280
[George] Inside five-story department stores, merchandise burst into flame through shattered glass windows before exterior walls even showed scorch marks.

46
00:07:39.280 --> 00:07:50.400
[George] At 1.30 in the morning, the wooden cupola of the Cook County Courthouse ignited, sending its five-ton bronze bell crashing through six floors into the basement in a shower of sparks.

47
00:07:50.400 --> 00:08:04.160
[George] Thousands of panicked refugees jammed the bridges across the Chicago River, clutching children, bundles of clothing, and caged birds, while horses bolted through crowds in terror under sky's raining fire and burning embers.

48
00:08:04.160 --> 00:08:14.960
[George] The radiant heat was so intense that horse hooves stuck to melting asphalt pavements, and wooden sailing schooners anchored in the river ignited before their crews could cast off their moorings.

49
00:08:14.960 --> 00:08:21.600
[George] Shortly after three o'clock in the morning on October 9th, the ultimate death blow struck Chicago.

50
00:08:21.600 --> 00:08:33.040
[George] A burning timber beam, carried aloft by the cyclonic wind, landed on the pine shingle roof of the municipal waterworks on Pine Street, more than a mile north of the main fire front.

51
00:08:33.040 --> 00:08:40.880
[George] Within twenty minutes, the roof collapsed into the engine room, crushing the steam-powered pumping engines and flooding the facility.

52
00:08:40.880 --> 00:08:46.080
[George] Instantly, every fire hydrant across the entire city ran completely dry.

53
00:08:46.080 --> 00:08:50.880
[George] Firefighters dropped their useless hoses in despair and fled for their lives.

54
00:08:50.880 --> 00:08:55.280
[George] With no water, the fire became an absolute master of the city.

55
00:08:55.280 --> 00:09:05.880
[George] It marched through the wealthy north side mansions of Lincoln Park and the working class tenements alike, creating a wall of flame two miles wide that devoured entire blocks in seconds.

56
00:09:06.360 --> 00:09:19.640
[George] Over thirty thousand desperate refugees fled to the sandy shores of Lake Michigan, standing neck deep in the freezing surf for hours to escape the searing radiant heat and flying embers that set their hair and clothing on fire.

57
00:09:19.640 --> 00:09:31.560
[George] Mothers held crying babies above the waves while ships offshore illuminated the apocalyptic scene against the midnight sky, watching three square miles of an American metropolis burn to the waterline.

58
00:09:31.560 --> 00:09:42.680
[George] As the pumps ground to a halt, the hiss of steam gave way to silence, leaving the firefighters staring in disbelief as the city's last line of defense collapsed into glowing embers.

59
00:09:42.680 --> 00:09:50.280
[George] The inferno burned through Monday afternoon until late Monday night, October 9, when nature finally intervened.

60
00:09:50.280 --> 00:10:00.440
[George] A steady, gentle Midwestern autumn rain began to fall from the smoke-choked sky, gradually extinguishing the creeping flames and cooling the glowing piles of masonry.

61
00:10:00.440 --> 00:10:04.200
[George] The devastation revealed by Tuesday morning was staggering.

62
00:10:04.200 --> 00:10:13.720
[George] Three and a half square miles of central Chicago were reduced to smoking fields of ash, twisted iron streetcar rails, and solitary brick chimneys.

63
00:10:13.720 --> 00:10:17.960
[George] Over 17,500 buildings were completely destroyed.

64
00:10:17.960 --> 00:10:25.400
[George] At least 300 people had lost their lives, though the true toll was likely far higher due to incinerated remains.

65
00:10:25.400 --> 00:10:36.760
[George] More than 100,000 residents, nearly one third of the city population, were left homeless with only the clothes on their backs, camping in muddy prairie fields and church basements.

66
00:10:36.760 --> 00:10:53.040
[George] Relief trains carrying bread, blankets, and drinking water soon poured in from New York, London, and cities across the globe, sparking the most massive international humanitarian response in American history and demonstrating the global affection for the fallen city.

67
00:10:53.040 --> 00:11:04.960
[George] Families spent days searching makeshift bulletin boards and church registries for lost children, while telegraph offices were overwhelmed by millions of inquiries from frantic relatives across the globe.

68
00:11:04.960 --> 00:11:09.280
[George] Even before the ashes cooled, the search for a scapegoat began.

69
00:11:09.280 --> 00:11:20.960
[George] Chicago Republican reporter Michael Ahern fabricated a colorful story that Catherine O'Leary had been milking her cow in the dark when the beast kicked over a kerosene lantern, setting the barn ablaze.

70
00:11:20.960 --> 00:11:30.720
[George] The sensational lie played directly into anti-Irish and anti-Catholic prejudices of the era, and newspapers worldwide repeated the tale as gospel truth.

71
00:11:30.720 --> 00:11:42.320
[George] Catherine O'Leary was hounded by reporters, received death threats, and lived out the remainder of her life as a virtual recluse in profound sorrow, weeping whenever the fire was mentioned.

72
00:11:42.320 --> 00:11:53.600
[George] It was not until 1997, more than a century after her death, that the Chicago City Council passed an official resolution fully exonerating Catherine O'Leary and her cow.

73
00:11:53.600 --> 00:11:58.640
[George] Meanwhile, Chicago rose from its ashes with unprecedented speed and vigor.

74
00:11:58.640 --> 00:12:12.720
[George] Banning wooden construction in the downtown core, architects pioneered steel frame construction and fireproof terracotta cladding, berthing the modern skyscraper and establishing Chicago as the architectural capital of the modern world.

75
00:12:12.720 --> 00:12:20.400
[George] Today, the Chicago Fire Academy stands proudly at 137 DeCoven Street, on the exact site of Mrs.

76
00:12:20.400 --> 00:12:20.560
[George] O.

77
00:12:20.640 --> 00:12:27.360
[George] Leary Barn, training new generations of firefighters upon the soil where modern Chicago was born from fire.

78
00:12:50.040 --> 00:12:51.400
[Rebecca] I'm Rebecca.

79
00:12:51.400 --> 00:13:07.400
[Rebecca] Join us next week as we explore the dazzling 1893 World's Columbian Exposition and the birth of the giant Ferris.

